#import "style.typ": *
#chapter[From automorphic representations to congruence lengths]

== Matching an eigenvalue at two places <c15s01>

At a place away from the level, commuting Hecke operators can be diagonalized on a suitable automorphic eigenspace. Their eigenvalues specify a polynomial. To compare congruences between eigenforms with global cohomology, one needs a Galois representation for which the matching Frobenius element has the corresponding characteristic polynomial. At a ramified place an ordinary polynomial need not carry enough information. A Weil–Deligne parameter includes both an action of the Weil group and a nilpotent monodromy operator. At a place above the coefficient prime the theorem also prescribes the jumps of a de Rham filtration for the stated local types.

The transfer theorem used here starts with a regular algebraic cuspidal representation $pi$ of $op("GL")_a$ over a CM field $E$, polarized by a Hecke character of its maximal real subfield. Regularity is a condition on the distinct infinitesimal weights at infinity. Polarization relates the conjugate representation to its dual with the specified character twist. For a prime $ℓ$ and an identification of an algebraic closure of $ℚ_ℓ$ with the complex numbers, the theorem constructs a continuous semisimple $a$-dimensional representation $r$ of the absolute Galois group of $E$.

The version used in the proof has carefully delimited compatibility conditions. Away from $ℓ$ the parameters have equal dimensions, agree in the toral zero-monodromy case, and have matching inertia characteristic polynomials in the following additional case. The automorphic parameter must be tame and square-free in the specified sense, with zero monodromy. The local base must be isomorphic to $ℚ_g$, with either $a=1$ or $g>16a^2$. The arithmetic parameter then also has zero monodromy. Above $ℓ$, at the compatible local comparison data in the theorem, a spherical component gives crystalline and de Rham restrictions with the specified jumps. The potentially semistable comparison requires zero monodromy together with either the toral condition or the complete tame, square-free and local-field size condition. If $z$ runs over the infinitesimal character at an embedding, the Hodge–Tate multiset in these conventions consists of $frac(a-1,2)-z$. At the conjugate embedding the weights are reflected about half the purity weight.

The normalizations can be checked in dimension one. For an unramified character a Frobenius polynomial has a single root. If Frobenius is replaced by its inverse, the root is inverted. Replacing the automorphic normalization by an algebraic normalization twists the roots by a power of the residue norm. In rank $a$ the shift of $frac(a-1,2)$ in the weights records that normalization. Congruence comparisons with two different Frobenius conventions would have no reason to match.

== A Hecke polynomial and a Galois polynomial <c15s02>

At a split unramified place of residue size $q$ the compact subgroup of integral matrices provides a reference vector. A double coset acts by summing translates of that vector. One can see the first rank-two operator on the lattices of a two-dimensional local vector space. Let $ϖ$ be a uniformizer and $L$ a free rank-two lattice over the valuation ring. The sublattices of index $q$ between $ϖ L$ and $L$ are the inverse images of the lines in $L slash ϖ L$. The number of lines is $frac(q^2-1,q-1)=q+1$. The unnormalized neighbor operator consequently sends a constant function on this lattice model to $(q+1)$ times the same function. An automorphic Hecke eigenvalue packages such sums after the chosen level and normalization have been fixed.

For a two-dimensional representation and a particular Galois element $g$, the polynomial is $X^2-tr(r(g))X+det(r(g))$. Trace alone at this one element does not determine its roots. A polynomial for every element of the Galois group, or the trace function on a dense system with suitable identities, carries much more information. The transfer theorem attaches one global representation whose restrictions satisfy the specified local comparisons. Writing down an unrelated companion matrix for the Hecke polynomial at each prime would impose no compatibility with multiplication in the global Galois group. Existence of the representation is therefore a deep step even when each individual polynomial has small degree.

The shift from automorphic normalization to algebraic normalization is explicit in the source. For rank $a$ it twists the local representation by the determinant norm to exponent $frac(1-a,2)$. The translation of the infinite-place infinitesimal character uses the matching expression $frac(a-1,2)-z$. In rank two a half-power occurs in both conventions. In rank one both shifts disappear. Replacing a local class field map by the inverse map would also invert every unramified root. A consistent comparison specifies the normalization of both sides before equating the parameters. #src("Development.AbelianVarietyTheory.PublishedTheorems.GaloisCohomology₂.GaloisRepOfPolarizedCuspidal")[The algebraic normalization.]

For a numerical weight check, take the rank-three infinitesimal-character multiset $lr({5,1,-2})$ at one embedding. The indicated rule sends it to $lr({-4,0,3})$. If the purity weight in this example is $w=4$, the multiset required at the conjugate embedding is $lr({8,4,1})$. Reordering the entries preserves the relation. Reflection compares two embeddings, and a shift by one changes the cyclotomic twist in which an eventual extension appears. These numbers check the sign and the rank shift independently of the existence of an automorphic form with these weights.

== Reconstructing the polynomial from traces <c15s03>

In characteristic zero there is an explicit recovery of the characteristic polynomial of a single matrix from traces of powers of that same matrix. For eigenvalues $alpha_1,dots,alpha_a$, counted with multiplicities, write $e_k$ for the elementary symmetric function and $t_j=sum_i alpha_i^j$. Set $e_0=1$ and form the polynomial $E(T)=product_i (1+alpha_i T)=sum_k e_k T^k$. The logarithmic derivative has a simple formal expansion,
$ frac(E'(T),E(T))=sum_i frac(alpha_i,1+alpha_i T)
                 =sum_(j>=1)(-1)^(j-1)t_j T^(j-1). $
The inverse to $E$ exists in the formal power series ring because its constant term is one. Multiply back by $E$ and compare degree $k-1$. The result is
$ k e_k=sum_(j=1)^k (-1)^(j-1)e_(k-j)t_j. $
Thus $t_1,dots,t_a$ determine all coefficients of $det(X I-M)$. For a representation one obtains $t_j$ by evaluating its trace at $g^j$ whenever the trace is given as a function on the group. Replacing $t_j$ by the trace at an unrelated prime with similar residue size has no such justification.

For example, if $t_1=12,t_2=66,t_3=408$, the recursion gives $e_1=12$, $2e_2=12^2-66=78$, and $3e_3=39 dot 12-12 dot 66+408=84$. The resulting polynomial is $X^3-12X^2+39X-28=(X-1)(X-4)(X-7)$. The signs in its coefficients alternate because the calculation used $E(T)$ with plus signs. A representation with a nontrivial Jordan block can have the same trace data as its semisimplification. The identity matrix and a two-by-two upper unipotent matrix in characteristic zero have the same trace at every integral power, even though only the former acts trivially. This is the reason a trace comparison is formulated with appropriate semisimplicity or with a precise statement about which semisimplification is identified.

For a congruence modulo $ℓ^N$, division by $k$ in the recursion costs precision if $ℓ$ divides $k$. Already at rank two, $2e_2=t_1^2-t_2$. Knowledge of the right-hand side modulo $2^N$ yields $e_2$ modulo $2^(N-1)$ from this equation alone when $N>=1$. Division over a characteristic-zero local field is legitimate, but taking the rational result modulo a residue prime requires the lost digit to be accounted for first. In characteristic two the failure can be exact. Over the field of four elements choose a unit $u$ of order three. Let a generator of a cyclic group of order three act on a two-dimensional space by $I$ in one representation and by $u I$ in another. The trace of every group element is zero for both representations because the dimension is two in characteristic two. Their determinant characters differ at the generator, where they have values one and $u^2$. Their characteristic polynomials there are $X^2+1$ and $X^2+u^2$. Thus the passage through a characteristic-zero system, integral characteristic coefficients, and their specified limits can carry information lost by raw residual traces.

For one coefficient, compatible residues modulo all powers of the uniformizer have a unique limit in a complete discrete valuation ring. Extending an entire system of Frobenius traces requires a simultaneous estimate. In the source let $x_j$ be the sampled group elements, $theta_j$ the desired values in a fixed finite extension of $ℚ_ℓ$, and $eta_(i)(j)$ an approximating system which equals $tr(rho_(i)(x_j))$ for $j in D_i$. For any finite list $I$ of systems the set of $x_j$ with $j in inter.big_(i in I)D_i$ must be dense. Given a tolerance $delta>0$, there must be a *single* finite linear combination, with coefficients $c_i$, for which
$ abs(P(theta)-sum_(i in I)c_i P(eta_i))<=delta $
holds for every polynomial $P$ in the sampling coordinates with integral coefficients. In particular choose $P$ to be the coordinate $j$. On the common domain this bounds the difference between $theta_j$ and $sum_i c_i tr(rho_(i)(x_j))$. For two such approximations take every domain indexed by the union of their two finite supports. The samples in the intersection of all those domains are dense. There the two trace combinations differ by at most the larger of their tolerances by the ultrametric inequality. Their difference is continuous, so the same bound holds throughout the group. This explains how the approximation yields a uniform Cauchy system of trace functions. To find the values of the limit, first take it in the completed algebraic closure of $ℚ_ℓ$. Each trace combination is within its tolerance of the fixed finite coefficient field at every sample in its common domain, because $theta_j$ belongs to that field. Continuity of distance to the field extends the same bound to every group element. As the tolerances tend to zero, the limit has distance zero from that field. A finite extension of $ℚ_ℓ$ is complete and hence closed in the completed algebraic closure. The resulting function therefore takes its values in the prescribed field. Realization by a semisimple representation and the comparisons at local places occupy further steps. #src("Development.AbelianVarietyTheory.Definitions.GaloisGeometry.PolyApproxZeroPadic")[The polynomial approximation condition.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.Foundations.GaloisRepresentations.TraceLimits.ExistsTraceLimit")[The density and trace-limit conclusions.]

== Which local comparisons are used <c15s04>

A Weil–Deligne datum consists of a representation of the Weil group and a nilpotent operator $N$ obeying a scaling rule under that group. The inertia action measures finite ramification in the Weil representation; the operator records an additional unipotent part of the original Galois action. Equal Frobenius eigenvalues alone need not give the same pair. For instance take two-dimensional Weil data with unramified diagonal characters in the ratio required by the scaling rule. With a suitable choice of ordering, the matrix carrying the second coordinate into the first can be taken for $N$, while $N=0$ is another possible choice for the same underlying Weil action. The two nilpotent operators have ranks one and zero, respectively. Their ranks already distinguish the pairs.

The condition imposed away from $ℓ$ has three clauses. Write $x$ for the Weil–Deligne datum derived from the arithmetic representation and $y$ for the parameter of the algebraically normalized local automorphic component. Their dimensions must be equal. If the commutators of Weil elements act trivially on $y$ in the specified sense and $y$ has zero monodromy, the two parameters agree. For the additional tame case, the completion at the place must admit a continuous field identification with $ℚ_g$ for a prime $g$, and either the rank is one or $g>16 a^2$. When the tame and square-free conditions of the theorem hold and $N_y=0$, its conclusion is $N_x=0$ and equality of characteristic polynomials on every degree-zero Weil element. This last clause is an inertia comparison, possibly short of equality on all Weil elements. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.Foundations.GaloisRepresentations.TypeConditions.WeightsConjBis")[The three away conditions.]

In this statement the condition called square-free in the source has a specific allowance. For a tame inertia generator modulo wild inertia as specified there, a repeated root $z$ of its inertia polynomial is allowed only if $z^(q-1)=1$, where $q$ is the norm of the place. Requiring every eigenvalue to be distinct would be stronger. The wild condition asks for the polynomial $(X-1)^a$ on the indicated wild elements. In the semisimple setting it expresses the tame restriction in a way stable under comparison of polynomials. The size hypothesis concerns the *field* $E_w$ as well as the prime: residue characteristic bigger than $16a^2$ by itself does not assert that $E_w$ is $ℚ_g$. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.Foundations.GaloisRepresentations.TypeConditions.WeightsConj")[The inertia-polynomial condition.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.Foundations.GaloisRepresentations.TypeConditions.WeightsConjAlt")[Square-free and commutator conditions.]

Above $ℓ$ the theorem uses compatible period-ring data, including a nonempty local comparison datum and an inducing bridge at the place. For a spherical component the restriction is crystalline and de Rham, with specified jumps at each embedding induced via that bridge. For the other clause let $y$ be the parameter of the algebraically normalized local component. Require $N_y=0$ and either the toral condition on $y$ or the full conjunction of the large-local-field, tame and square-free conditions just described. Then the restriction is de Rham and potentially semistable, with the same prescribed jumps and the away-style comparison for its resulting Weil–Deligne parameter. A list of Hodge–Tate weights is therefore only part of the conclusion. Two representations may have the same weights and very different inertia or monodromy. Conversely, a Frobenius polynomial at one other prime contains no direct filtration at $ℓ$.

The order of quantifiers is useful when applying the theorem to the family of Chapter 16. Fix a CM number field, its algebraic closure, the coefficient prime $ℓ$, an identification of algebraically closed coefficient fields, a rank $a>=1$, and a regular algebraic cuspidal representation polarized by the prescribed Hecke character. The theorem supplies *one* continuous semisimple $r$, an integer $w$, and a multiset assignment $H_tau$, satisfying all of the local assertions for that datum. One must next account for allowed variation in the original automorphic object and in the coefficient prime when bounding congruences. No uniformity in the field or rank follows from this existence theorem without the quantitative integral comparisons. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.Foundations.GaloisRepresentations.TypeConditions.WeightsConjBis")[The full transfer statement.]

== How the transfer is supplied <c15s05>

The formal construction first gets geometric trace data in an integral situation. The geometric input is built from the Lefschetz formula for finite étale correspondences. Applied to a Hecke correspondence, the formula relates an alternating trace on cohomology to its fixed-point contributions. This supplies traces for the starting family. Congruences between algebraic weights lead to an approximation of the prescribed trace values by continuous trace systems. Their agreement on the relevant common dense subsets lets the limit extend to the Galois group. The passage uses semisimplicity and trace identities to identify the global representation that the local polynomials describe.

There are two extension steps. Base change and descent transport automorphic data through fields where local components are under better control. Rigidity at split places and multiplicity one identify the factors on descent. Large-rank definite theta lifts give the required changes of weights and rank. In this latter existence theorem a sufficiently large skew-Hermitian target, compatible splitting characters, and an inequality between the target weight and the source highest weights ensure the existence of an automorphic lift. The construction combines the lift, the trace congruences, and descent away from a finite set. It then establishes the spherical and Iwahori comparisons needed in passing to the strong local conditions stated above.

One elementary principle behind the descent is worth isolating. If two continuous characteristic polynomials of semisimple representations agree on Frobenius elements in a dense subset of the relevant Galois group, continuity extends the polynomial identities and the characteristic-zero character criterion identifies the semisimplifications. Density and compatible identifications on overlaps are essential here. A comparison only at a finite list of sample primes cannot perform this step.

The local Langlands ingredient is provided separately. One extends the correspondence on cuspidal data to general irreducibles using the segment classification and checks compatibility with twists, characters, and the relevant types. At auxiliary places one also needs cuspidal characters of finite general linear groups with controlled dimensions. In Green's parametrization a Frobenius orbit of regular degree-$d$ characters at residue-field size $q$ yields degree $product_(i=1)^(d-1)(q^i-1)$. Distinct regular orbits give distinct cuspidal representations. These facts supply the auxiliary local types and the dimension comparisons in the theta construction.

== Why the finite-field label is a Frobenius orbit <c15s06>

At auxiliary split places the local types include cuspidal representations of $op("GL")_(d)(𝔽_q)$. In Green's parametrization the labels for cuspidals come from regular characters of $𝔽_(q^d)^times$, taken modulo Frobenius. A character is regular if its orbit under $z mapsto z^q$ has size $d$. Frobenius-conjugate characters label the same representation. Distinct regular *orbits* give distinct cuspidals. A single cuspidal in this parametrization has degree $product_(i=1)^(d-1)(q^i-1)$. These facts supply local dimensions and distinguish the types appearing in an inertial parameter. #src("Development.AbelianVarietyTheory.Proofs.InertiaEigenvalues.GreenCuspidalCharsByRegularCharsProof")[The regular-character parametrization.]

For $q=3$ and $d=2$, choose a generator of $𝔽_9^times$ and label its characters by exponents $a$ modulo eight. Frobenius takes $a$ to $3a$ modulo eight. The fixed exponents are zero and four. The remaining exponents form the three pairs
$ {1,3}, quad {2,6}, quad {5,7}. $
There are therefore three regular orbits. Each gives a degree-two cuspidal. Counting the six individual regular characters would double the number of distinct local types. At $q=2,d=3$ the exponents are modulo seven and Frobenius acts by doubling. The regular orbits are $lr({1,2,4})$ and $lr({3,6,5})$. Their degrees are $(2-1)(2^2-1)=3$. The rank of the ambient general linear group and the dimension of the representation of its finite group of points are thus different numbers. Both kinds of dimension are used in comparing lifts.

There is also a quick count of regular labels in degree two. The character group has order $q^2-1$. Frobenius fixes exactly the exponents satisfying $(q-1)a equiv 0 mod (q^2-1)$, of which there are $q-1$. Hence $q^2-q$ exponents have full orbit, yielding $frac(q(q-1),2)$ cuspidal labels. This elementary computation complements the representation-theoretic theorem asserting that all cuspidals occur and that the dimensions above are correct. In higher composite degree, subtracting only the fixed points would leave shorter nontrivial orbits; Möbius inversion on the divisors of $d$ accounts for them.

In the construction of the local correspondence the cuspidal parametrization is extended to all irreducible local inputs through segments. The extension agrees with the original map on cuspidals, respects the rank, and satisfies the stated compatibilities with norm twists, duality, and the relevant types. In rank one the local class field character supplies its value. These compatibility checks matter because changing the algebraic normalization first and then applying the correspondence must agree with the corresponding change on the parameter. For the arithmetic application the local map is needed with exactly the compatibilities entering the global transfer described above. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.Foundations.LocalLanglands.LocalCentralUnits.RecTExists")[Construction and properties of the local parameter.]

One can count the shorter orbits systematically. If $s$ divides $d$, the exponents modulo $q^d-1$ fixed by the $s$th power of Frobenius satisfy $(q^s-1)a equiv 0 mod (q^d-1)$. There are $q^s-1$ of them because $q^s-1$ divides $q^d-1$. Write $R_h$ for the number of exponents of least orbit length $h$. Then $q^s-1=sum_(h divides s) R_h$. Möbius inversion gives $R_d=sum_(s divides d) mu(d/s)(q^s-1)$. The number of regular labels is $frac(R_d,d)$. At $q=2,d=4$ the proper orbit lengths are one and two. The fixed set of the square already contains all the shorter orbits, and it has three elements among the fifteen exponents. The remaining twelve form three regular orbits of four. Each associated cuspidal has dimension $(2-1)(2^2-1)(2^3-1)=21$. Subtracting only the single exponent fixed by ordinary Frobenius would leave fourteen, a count that cannot even be divided into orbits of four.

== Congruence information that a rational trace can forget <c15s07>

Even when a rational representation is known, congruences depend on its integral models. Take an odd prime $ℓ$, let $k>=1$, put $u=1+ℓ^k$, and give the topological generator of a procyclic $ℤ_ℓ$-group eigenvalues $1,u$. In a basis $e_1,e_2$ it acts diagonally. A second stable lattice has basis $e_1,e'_2$ with $e'_2=frac(e_2-e_1,ℓ^k)$. In this basis its matrix is
$ mat(1,1;0,u). $
Indeed $g e'_2=u e'_2+e_1$. The representation extends continuously to all $n in ℤ_ℓ$ using $u^n$; the off-diagonal entry is $frac(u^n-1,ℓ^k)$. Each lattice has the same characteristic polynomial at every element of the group. Their reductions at the generator are the identity matrix and a nontrivial unipotent Jordan block. In particular the second reduction gives a nonsplit extension of the trivial residual character by itself. Traces recover the rational semisimple system but can lose the precise extension measured by an integral congruence.

The general cocycle calculation makes this precise. For diagonal characters $alpha,beta$, write the upper-right entry at $g$ as $b(g)$. The group law implies
$ b(g h)=alpha(g)b(h)+b(g)beta(h). $
Dividing by $beta(g h)$ shows that $c(g)=frac(b(g),beta(g))$ satisfies the cocycle relation with coefficient character $alpha beta^(-1)$. Changing the lift of the lower basis vector alters $c$ by a coboundary. If the two characters arise by twisting through powers of the cyclotomic character, their ratio records the difference of weights. This is one source of the positive twist index $h$ in an extension bound. The actual comparison uses the selected integral automorphic lattices, their Hecke congruences, and local admissibility of extensions; a bare count of possible characteristic polynomials cannot replace it.

For a finite group $G$ with order invertible in the coefficient ring, the averaging argument explains a simplification. Let $c$ be a one-cocycle with values in a $G$-module $M$, and let $v=|G|^(-1)sum_(h in G)c(h)$. Because left multiplication permutes $G$,
$ |G| v=sum_h c(g h)=|G| c(g)+g (|G|v). $
Thus $c(g)=v-g v$ and its cohomology class vanishes. This calculation gives an integral proof precisely when division by the group order is allowed. Over $ℤ_ℓ$ it works for the prime-to-$ℓ$ part, while the $ℓ$-part can carry torsion extension classes as in the example. The primary decomposition used in the next chapter follows this distinction.

The strong local–global implication takes a geometric trace input and a large-rank theta-lifting input. The later integral estimate additionally selects lattices and local conditions on extensions. Those choices are responsible for the distinction between the two residual lattices in the example. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.Foundations.GaloisRepresentations.IntegralStructures.Development")[The two inputs to the strong transfer.]

== Reading local restrictions from cocycles <c15s08>

Consider first a finite cyclic group $C$ of order $d$ with generator $g$, acting on a module $M$. A one-cocycle $c$ satisfies $c(g h)=c(g)+g c(h)$. It is determined by $m=c(g)$. Induction gives
$ c(g^i)=(1+g+dots+g^(i-1))m $
for every positive $i$. The relation $g^d=1$ forces $N m=0$, where $N=1+g+dots+g^(d-1)$. Conversely any $m$ in this kernel gives a cocycle via the formula. Reducing the exponent modulo $d$ is well defined because adding a full cycle contributes a translate of $N m$, which vanishes. A coboundary coming from $v in M$ takes the value $(g-1)v$ at the generator, up to the harmless choice of a simultaneous sign convention. It follows that
$ H^(1)(C,M)=ker(N) slash (g-1)M. $
For a trivial action this is $M[d]$, the subgroup annihilated by $d$. With $M=ℤ slash 3ℤ$ and $d=3$ it is the full additive group. For the same cyclic group acting trivially on $ℤ_3$ it vanishes. With $M=ℚ_3 slash ℤ_3$ it has length one over $ℤ_3$. Changing between the compact lattice, its rational vector space and its torsion quotient can therefore change the extension group even when all three have the same trivial scalar action.

Restriction to a subgroup has a concrete effect. If $h$ divides $d$, restriction to the cyclic subgroup generated by $g^h$ sends the class of $m$ to the class of $(1+g+dots+g^(h-1))m$. With trivial action this is simply $h m$. For $C$ of order nine, $M=ℤ slash 9ℤ$, and $h=3$, the subgroup cohomology is $M[3]$ and restriction is the surjection $m mapsto 3m$ onto that order-three subgroup. Its kernel also has order three. Requiring local restriction to vanish can thus impose fewer conditions than forcing the original class to vanish, even when every group is cyclic. Requiring only that restriction to inertia vanish can impose still fewer conditions than triviality on the whole decomposition group.

For the actual number field choose a decomposition group $D_v$ and its inertia subgroup $I_v$ at each finite place. There are different legitimate subspaces of $H^(1)(D_v,M)$ one can allow. The zero subspace requires the entire local class to vanish. The unramified subspace is the kernel of further restriction from $D_v$ to $I_v$. The full subspace imposes no condition there. For chosen subspaces $L_v$, the Selmer condition is the inverse image of all the $L_v$ under restriction. If the local spaces are enlarged from $L_v$ to $L'_v$ at a finite set of places and agree elsewhere, restriction followed by projection gives an injection
$ op("Sel")(L') slash op("Sel")(L) arrow.r product_v L'_v slash L_v. $
Indeed its kernel comprises exactly the classes whose restrictions already belonged to all the original spaces. This supplies a bound on the change of length when the objects on the right have finite length. There is no implied surjectivity onto arbitrary local data. Global classes must satisfy compatibility across the different places. #src("Formalism.GaloisCohomology.Statement")[Local conditions, Selmer modules, and inflation.]

The pinned rule for these related Selmer groups gives the chosen $ℓ$-adic local space at a place above $ℓ$, the full space at an allowed exceptional place away from $ℓ$, and the unramified space at the remaining finite places. The strict kernel whose length enters the congruence estimate has a more specific description. It starts with cohomology of the maximal extension unramified outside $S$ and imposes zero local restriction at the primes in $S$. Inflation into the unrestricted global cohomology is injective under the hypotheses of the inflation theorem. Its image consists exactly of the classes whose restrictions to inertia vanish at every prime outside $S$ and every prolongation over it. This is the link between imposing a ramification condition on the *extension defining the global group* and recognizing the same condition on an individual class of the absolute Galois group. Allowing an exceptional prime into a local comparison or changing a zero condition to an unramified condition changes the module whose length is being counted. The duality identity and the finite-sum bound must therefore be applied to the specified module with its specified set $S$.

== A localization kernel bounds a congruence loss <c15s09>

For a positive integer twist $h$ the relevant global classes have coefficients $M_h=(ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ)(-h)$ and vanish under restriction at the places over $ℓ$. The integral dual coefficient in degree two is $ℤ_(ℓ)(h+1)$. Local and global duality compare the length of the restriction kernel with a degree-two length and with the invariants at the global and local fields. These local corrections are important even for a totally imaginary field. The calculation with the localization map below gives their signs.

The index $j$ in the congruence expense $eta_j$ designates a lift. At that lift the comparison involves *all* positive twists $h<t$ and all coefficient primes. The common degree factor $D_0$ is the small-level dimension obtained from a separate rank identity for the lifts. Chapter 16 supplies finiteness of the required groups and a common bound $sqrt(b)$ for the logarithmically weighted sum on its chosen interval. Combining that bound with the integral comparison gives the division estimate used in Chapter 14. We derive this quantitative consequence after accounting for the local restrictions and their lengths.

== Taking lengths in the localization sequence <c15s10>

Write $M_h=(ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ)(-h)$ for $h>=1$ and $T_h=ℤ_(ℓ)(h+1)$. The finite local set $S$ consists exactly of the places above $ℓ$. At every place of $S$ one fixes a prolongation to the algebraic closure in order to define restriction. Different choices conjugate the local data and preserve the lengths in the theorem. The global classes are taken on $G_(E,S)$, already enforcing the unramified condition at the finite places outside $S$. The restriction kernel is
$ cal("K")=ker(H^(1)(G_(E,S),M_h) arrow.r product_(v in S) H^(1)(E_v,M_h)). $
The dual Tate twist of $M_h$ is $T_h$ because a local Tate pairing has total twist one, and $(-h)+(h+1)=1$. The shift by one is compulsory even for the first positive index $h=1$.

Let $B=H^(2)(G_(E,S),T_h)$ and $C=product_(v in S)H^(2)(E_v,T_h)$. Set $K=ker(B arrow.r C)$ and $I=op("im")(B arrow.r C)$. For finite lengths,
$ len B=len K+len I, quad len C=len I+len(C slash I). $
Global duality identifies the *length* of $K$ with that of $cal("K")$ and identifies the length of $C slash I$ with that of $H^(0)(E,M_h)$. Local duality identifies each local $H^2$ length with its $H^(0)(M_h)$ length. Adding to eliminate $I$ gives
$ len B+len H^(0)(E,M_h)=len cal("K")+sum_(v in S)len H^(0)(E_v,M_h). $
The equality is also packaged in extended natural lengths, but subtraction is appropriate only after establishing finiteness. The global duality case used in the source is $ℓ!=2$ or $E$ has no real places. A CM field satisfies this condition also at $ℓ=2$. This separates an archimedean qualification from the list of finite primes in $S$. #src("Formalism.GaloisCohomology.Statement")[The localization maps, duality hypotheses, and length formula.]

The invariant terms can often be understood from a scalar computation. On $ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ$ a scalar $u in ℤ_ℓ^times$ fixes precisely the kernel of multiplication by $u-1$. If $v_(ℓ)(u-1)=a < infinity$, that kernel is $ℓ^(-a)ℤ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ$ and has length $a$. For a group of scalars, simultaneous invariants are the intersection of these kernels, with length the minimum of their finite valuations if such a valuation occurs. In the twist $M_h$ use $u=kappa(g)^(-h)$. At an odd prime, if one element of the cyclotomic image is $1+ℓ^r$ with $r>=1$, then $v_(ℓ)((1+ℓ^r)^h-1)=r+v_(ℓ)(h)$. The same valuation holds for the inverse power. This gives a finite explicit bound for the invariants, growing only at special twists. At two the binomial valuation requires extra care for a shallow unit, another reason the two-primary tower receives separate treatment.

As a check on the signs, suppose the length of $B$ is six, one global invariant contributes length one, and two local invariant lengths are two and one. Then the kernel length is four. The map $B arrow.r C$ has target of length three, cokernel of length one, and image of length two; hence its kernel indeed has length four. Forgetting the cokernel correction would give three instead of four. Reversing both invariant corrections would give eight. Local invariants represent part of the information already seen by restriction. Their length is not automatically a new global congruence cost.

The invariant-length computation at odd $ℓ$ can be proved directly from the binomial theorem. If $x=1+u$ with $v_(ℓ)(u)=r>=1$, and a positive integer $a$ is prime to $ℓ$, the linear term $a u$ in $x^a-1$ has valuation $r$ while all higher terms have valuation at least $2r$. Now raise $x$ to the $ℓ$th power. The linear term $ℓ u$ has valuation $r+1$. The terms of degrees $2$ through $ℓ-1$ have one further factor of $ℓ$ and valuation at least $2r+1$. The last term has valuation $ℓ r>r+1$ for odd $ℓ$. Thus raising to the $ℓ$th power increases the valuation by exactly one. Writing $h=a ℓ^k$ and iterating proves $v_(ℓ)(x^h-1)=r+k$. Since $x$ is a unit, replacing $h$ by $-h$ leaves the valuation unchanged. This last equality explains why negative Tate twists use the same elementary invariant estimate.

At two the last term in the square can have the same valuation as its linear term when $r=1$. The example $x=3$ exhibits the entire correction. If $h$ is odd, $3^h$ is three modulo four, so $v_(2)(3^h-1)=1$. When $h=2k$, write $3^h=9^k$. Since $9-1=8$, its initial valuation is three. An odd power preserves it by the same linear-term argument. Each subsequent squaring adds exactly one, since $v_(2)(y+1)=1$ whenever $y$ is one modulo four. Therefore for even $h$ the answer is $3+v_(2)(k)=2+v_(2)(h)$. The lengths for $h=1,2,4$ are respectively one, three and four. These are the fixed lengths for the one scalar $3$ on the twisted torsion module. Taking invariants under a larger decomposition group intersects this subgroup with the kernels for its other scalars and may decrease the answer.

There is a simple sign test of this last distinction. If the scalar image includes $-1$ and $h$ is odd, its action on the twist is still $-1$. Its invariants in $ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ$ are killed by two. At odd $ℓ$ the only such element is zero. At two the possible invariants form a subgroup of length at most one. For even $h$, this particular scalar gives no restriction at all. The argument uses the presence of $-1$ in the relevant local or global image as a hypothesis. A statement that only supplies a positive-depth unit does not automatically supply this sign, even when both calculations take place at the same rational coefficient prime.

== Recovering the numerical congruence budget <c15s11>

For each step $j$ the formal congruence sum is a weighted sum over the finite coefficient places at which the local character has changed. If $K$ is the coefficient field, write $lambda_w=frac(log(N w),[K:ℚ])$ and let $e_w$ be its ramification index over the rational residue prime. Then
$ eta_j=sum_(w " twisted") lambda_w e_w eta_(j,w). $
Here $eta_(j,w)$ is the local numerical expense recorded in the data. This weight has no leading two, unlike the $2lambda_w e_w$ multiplying the valuation gains in Chapter 13. The two definitions are inputs to different comparisons. Replacing one by the other for superficial consistency would change a quantified estimate. #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.GlobalTheoremInputs")[The weighted congruence term.]

Let $B=sqrt(b)$ and assume that each relevant localization kernel has finite length and that *every finite set* $P$ of rational primes satisfies
$ sum_(ℓ in P)(log ℓ)sum_(1 <= h < t)ell_(ℓ,h)<=B. $
For a fixed prime let $s_ℓ$ be its summand and set $s_n=0$ at composite positive integers and at one. All terms are nonnegative. The increasing partial sums $sum_(n<=R)s_n$ are bounded above by $B$, hence have a finite limit. Every sum over a nonempty finite set of primes is bounded by a partial sum ending at its largest prime. It follows that the infinite sum exists independently of the order of summation and is at most $B$. The source first uses the finiteness premise to identify the finite integer lengths in the lattice record with the formerly extended lengths. It then makes precisely this passage from finite sums to a summable nonnegative family. Without finiteness, converting a formal infinite length to an integer would not justify the equality. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.DivinNOfHypV")[The passage from lengths to the prime sum.]

In this application the summability conclusion even forces finite support. If the integer sum of lengths at a prime is positive, its summand is at least $log ℓ>=log 2$. A bounded increasing sum can contain at most $floor(frac(B,log 2))$ such primes, and each must satisfy $ℓ<=exp(B)$. This elementary observation does not choose the primes in advance. The cohomological theorem controls all finite subsets before one knows where a nonzero congruence occurs.

Once the twist-prime series is summable, the estimate linking transfer and congruences reads
$ eta_j <=d_j ( C (f m b L_x^2 +(log x)^2 N_c)+sum_n s_n). $
The fixed smaller-level comparison supplies $d_j=D_0$ for all steps in the applicable range. The other input is $B_0=f m b x^epsilon$, with $N_c>=0$ and $L_x=x^epsilon$. Write $y=x^epsilon>=1$. Since $f,m>=1$ and $b>=1$, we have $sqrt(b)<=b<=B_0<=y(B_0+N_c)$. For $x$ above the threshold depending on $epsilon$, $(log x)^2<=y$. Consequently
$ f m b L_x^2=y B_0, quad (log x)^2 N_c<=y N_c, $
$ eta_j <= D_0 (|C|+1)y(B_0+N_c). $
The absolute value handles a constant not assumed positive externally. All uses of $N_c$ are with nonnegative coefficients. No factor equal to the number of twists is appended at this point: that sum was already taken in $s_ℓ$ and bounded by $sqrt(b)$.

For a direct proof of the logarithmic inequality, set $u=log x$. For positive $epsilon$, $u^2 e^(-epsilon u)$ tends to zero. For example the degree-three term of the exponential series gives $u^2 e^(-epsilon u)<=frac(6,epsilon^3 u)$ for $u>0$. This proves eventual domination explicitly. If the family of primes, steps or embeddings forced $epsilon$ to vary with $x$, this elementary argument would require a different uniformity statement. In the application $epsilon$ is chosen once after specifying a putative zero, and the finite maximum of the thresholds in the integral and cohomological hypotheses is permissible.

There is a useful dimension check at the last comparison. If the datum $d_j$ were only bounded by a multiple of $D_0$ growing with $j$ or with the outer scale, dividing total degrees by $D_0$ could leave an unwanted factor. The established identity equates the *small-level* dimension to the reference dimension. Twisted-level spaces may have genuinely different dimensions, and their changes are among the quantities compared through congruences. Equality for every twisted dimension would be a much stronger assertion. The precise small-level identity is uniform in the host at scale $x$ and the step $1<=j<=m$. #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.GlobalTheoremInputs")[SmallLevelRankEq and the local comparison.] #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.DivinNOfHypV")[The quantitative deduction.]

The resulting bound feeds into division by the common period because an integral change of lattice with local length $a$ changes the logarithm of the index by $a log abs(k)$ over a local ring with residue field $k$. For example an $ℓ$-primary finite module $ℤ_ℓ slash ℓ^r ℤ_ℓ ⊕ ℤ_ℓ slash ℓ^s ℤ_ℓ$ has length $r+s$ and logarithmic cardinality $(r+s)log ℓ$. An inequality for a possible dimension of a tangent space, or for the number of distinct eigenvalues in characteristic zero, would not by itself control these exponents. The passage from automorphic congruences to integral extension lengths is what puts the Galois representation into the same units as the geometric determinant estimate. The next chapter obtains those length bounds simultaneously on a whole interval while allowing the field and the twists to vary within it.

